SYSTEM DESIGN IMPLEMENTATION
1. URL Shortener (Bit.ly)
Introduction
A URL Shortener is a system that converts a long URL into a short URL. When the user clicks the short URL, it redirects to the original website.
Functional Requirements
•	Convert long URL into short URL. 
•	Redirect users to the original URL. 
•	Handle billions of URLs. 
•	Support fast redirection. 
Non-Functional Requirements
•	High availability. 
•	Low latency. 
•	Scalable. 
•	Reliable. 
How it Works
1.	User enters a long URL. 
2.	Server generates a unique short code (for example: abc123). 
3.	Store the mapping in the database. 
4.	When someone clicks the short URL: 
o	Check Redis cache first. 
o	If found, redirect immediately. 
o	Otherwise, fetch from the database and redirect. 



Key Design Decisions
•	Base62 Encoding creates short and unique URLs. 
•	Redis Cache stores popular URLs for faster access. 
•	Database (Cassandra/DynamoDB) stores URL mappings. 
•	HTTP 301 for permanent redirect or 302 for temporary redirect. 
Challenge
Generating unique short URLs for billions of links.

2. Rate Limiter
Introduction
"A Rate Limiter controls how many requests a user can send in a specific time. It protects the server from abuse and overload."
Functional Requirements
•	Limit requests by user, IP, or API key. 
•	Reject requests after the limit. 
Non-Functional Requirements
•	Fast response. 
•	High availability. 
•	Distributed. 
How it Works
1.	Every request reaches the API Gateway. 
2.	Redis stores the request count. 
3.	If the count is below the limit, the request is allowed. 
4.	If the limit is exceeded, return HTTP 429 – Too Many Requests. 
Key Design Decisions
•	Token Bucket algorithm allows small bursts. 
•	Redis stores counters because it is very fast. 
•	Place the limiter at the API Gateway to protect all services. 

3. Chat Application (WhatsApp)
Introduction
"A Chat Application allows users to send and receive messages instantly. It also supports group chats, media sharing, and read receipts."
Functional Requirements
•	One-to-one chat. 
•	Group chat. 
•	Read receipts. 
•	Media sharing. 
•	Offline message storage. 
Non-Functional Requirements
•	Real-time communication. 
•	High availability. 
•	Scalable. 
•	Reliable. 
How it Works
1.	Users connect using WebSocket. 
2.	Sender sends a message. 
3.	Server stores the message. 
4.	If the receiver is online, the message is delivered instantly. 
5.	If offline, it is stored and delivered when the receiver comes online. 
Key Design Decisions
•	WebSocket provides real-time communication. 
•	Cassandra stores messages because it handles many writes. 
•	S3/Object Storage stores images and videos. 
•	Only the media link is sent in the chat. 

4. Zero-Trust Adaptive Access Control System
Problem Statement
Design a security system that continuously verifies users and devices before allowing access to organizational applications.
Instead of:
Login once → trusted forever
system follows:
Never automatically trust → continuously verify.
Functional Requirements
•	User authentication 
•	Device verification 
•	Risk assessment 
•	Application access control 
•	MFA when required 
•	Continuous session monitoring 
•	Automatic session revocation 


Non-Functional Requirements
•	High security 
•	Low latency 
•	High availability 
•	Scalability 
•	Auditability
